WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.564
As long as man has told stories, there has been folklore, tall tales,

00:00:03.564 --> 00:00:05.594
and in the modern age, urban legends.

00:00:05.642 --> 00:00:09.369
More recently, the advent of the Internet has resulted in an explosion of stories

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:11.082
often referred to as creepypasta.

00:00:11.146 --> 00:00:14.158
The most well known creepypastas, such as Slenderman, Jeff the Killer, and

00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:16.894
the Russian Sleep Experiment, were usually fictional stories

00:00:16.894 --> 00:00:19.706
originally created by anonymous Internet users using a pseudonym.

00:00:19.738 --> 00:00:22.414
But the lore is oftentimes built upon by the Internet community.

00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:25.974
Usually the result is that the original author loses control of his or her creation

00:00:26.012 --> 00:00:30.000
and that seems to be what happens in 2012 in the case of Smiley.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.454
Usually that only happens when the story is good to begin with,

00:00:32.454 --> 00:00:34.978
but Smiley has proven that there are exceptions.

00:00:34.978 --> 00:00:36.145
To learn how YouTubers made

00:00:36.145 --> 00:00:39.802
a horror movie so controversial that it had to be wiped off the face of the Internet, 

00:00:39.802 --> 00:00:41.978
and how the legacy of Smiley still persists today.

00:00:42.064 --> 00:00:44.380
Stick around to the end of this video.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:50.117
♪ Metal Music ♪

00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:51.750
Welcome to Horror History.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.150
(sigh)

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.964
I did this one for the lulz.

00:00:54.964 --> 00:00:58.718
Smiley is the 2012 horror masterpiece directed by Michael Gallagher,

00:00:58.724 --> 00:01:00.970
Who created the OG YouTube channel Totally Sketch.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.710
As you can tell by the name, it was a sketch comedy collective

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.074
 in the era of oversaturated and racy thumbnails.

00:01:06.122 --> 00:01:08.754
In the early days on YouTube, it was a really big deal and would often

00:01:08.792 --> 00:01:11.582
feature guest appearances from some of the biggest creators on the Internet.

00:01:11.646 --> 00:01:15.607
Michael also has an impressive resume of viral videos he directed for other channels,

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:18.254
and his connection to big creators seems to be the entire impetus

00:01:18.302 --> 00:01:20.658
behind the creation of his first narrative feature.

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:22.802
Well, okay, I won't say the entire impetus.

00:01:22.866 --> 00:01:25.986
Smiley deals with themes surrounding the pitfalls of online stranger danger

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:28.726
and the darkest communities on the web, something that Gallagher is

00:01:28.748 --> 00:01:31.618
qualified to make a movie about being an Internet creator himself.

00:01:31.714 --> 00:01:35.719
There are at least ten YouTubers or YouTube adjacent people in this movie that I spotted,

00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:37.578
which I'll point out as they come up in the story.

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:41.786
However, I too am an Internet creator and for that reason I am qualified to

00:01:41.808 --> 00:01:42.362
talk about it.

00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:43.834
But I am not the first.

00:01:43.952 --> 00:01:45.742
-[Dice] They dropped the ball pretty early on.

00:01:45.796 --> 00:01:48.782
-[Nick] If you want to see a movie that's boring, I got just the thing for you.

00:01:48.836 --> 00:01:49.770
-[Elvis] It's so stupid.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.914
-[Sixteenleo] Just maybe probably the worst plot that I've ever seen in a movie.

00:01:53.962 --> 00:01:56.010
-[Shane] Don't tell anybody I was in this sh*t movie.

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:56.826
-[BigWill] Yeah, it sucked.

00:01:56.858 --> 00:01:57.362
End of video.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:58.130
Thanks for watching.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.551
But does Smiley deserve all the hate?

00:02:00.551 --> 00:02:03.745
Is there actually something to this movie that others aren't seeing?

00:02:03.745 --> 00:02:08.000
How can a character who is still being celebrated eleven years later really be that bad? 

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.898
To understand Smiley, we've got to take it back to the group that turned Smiley

00:02:10.898 --> 00:02:13.750
from a scary story to supernatural stalker.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:14.294
(IMPACT)

00:02:14.294 --> 00:02:18.250
♪ Upbeat Music ♪

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.942
Most good movies are able to thrive on the strength of a captivating script,

00:02:22.006 --> 00:02:25.158
dynamic characters, stellar acting, and innovative directing.

00:02:25.254 --> 00:02:29.146
Most masterclasses of cinema do not need to open with a nice ass shot to

00:02:29.168 --> 00:02:30.278
catch the viewer's attention.

00:02:30.374 --> 00:02:31.510
But there are exceptions.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:36.000
I am, of course, talking about Lost in Translation, but Smiley can live up to that too, right?

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.586
This is Stacy, and she's the first on our YouTuber count

00:02:38.618 --> 00:02:41.498
because she's played by Nikki Limo from Totally Sketch.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:42.462
Okay, we get it.

00:02:42.516 --> 00:02:44.650
This is the only way they know how to hold the viewers' attention.

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.646
Stacy is part of a group of hackers from the online collective Anonymous

00:02:47.678 --> 00:02:50.578
who have gotten together at Pacific Valley University in Los Angeles to

00:02:50.584 --> 00:02:51.951
start a new viral trend.

00:02:51.951 --> 00:02:55.034
Using the Internet, they spread rumors of a killer named Smiley,

00:02:55.034 --> 00:02:59.578
a man who supposedly stitched his eyes shut and carved himself a permanent smile with a knife.

00:02:59.694 --> 00:03:03.628
-[Mary] Start a video chat with somebody you don't even know, and you type in: 

00:03:04.071 --> 00:03:05.001
I did it for the lulz.

00:03:06.248 --> 00:03:07.276
Three times.

00:03:07.276 --> 00:03:11.151
And then this guy with the knife appears behind them.

00:03:11.151 --> 00:03:12.580
-[Stacy] And what?

00:03:13.157 --> 00:03:14.506
-[Mary] They die?

00:03:14.528 --> 00:03:18.118
So the anonymous group seems to be staging some videos where users on both ends

00:03:18.134 --> 00:03:21.082
are their members, and these videos naturally get spread around the college

00:03:21.136 --> 00:03:22.950
and the Internet, and the legend grows.

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:26.198
Other than Stacy, the Smiley group consists of seven other main players.

00:03:26.294 --> 00:03:29.598
Kels is played by Richard Ryan, who was also part of Totally Sketch,

00:03:29.604 --> 00:03:31.348
but is most known for his gun channel full mag,

00:03:31.348 --> 00:03:33.370
where he blew up smartphones and gaming consoles.

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.766
This guy, who is only known as Flasher, you'll see why in a minute,

00:03:36.788 --> 00:03:39.806
is played by Jason Horton, who is part of Totally Sketch and had a small solo

00:03:39.838 --> 00:03:42.670
career where he transitioned from sex bait comedy to true crime.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.018
Then there's Zane, the party guy who can often be spotted with two friends.

00:03:46.104 --> 00:03:49.682
Crash is played by Steve Greene from, you guessed it, Totally Sketch.

00:03:49.746 --> 00:03:53.238
Steve married Nikki Limo in 2017, and they currently do a podcast together.

00:03:53.324 --> 00:03:57.030
The other friend is named Decepticon and played by DeStorm Power.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.002
DeStorm was one of the biggest self made musicians on early YouTube.

00:04:00.066 --> 00:04:03.515
Roxy, also known as Proxy, seems to be secretly dating Zane,

00:04:03.515 --> 00:04:04.416
in the movie, that is.

00:04:04.416 --> 00:04:07.338
And finally, there's Binder, who's seemingly the leader of the group

00:04:07.344 --> 00:04:08.570
and the mastermind behind the plan.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.994
He's played by Shane Dawson, one of the biggest names in comedy then,

00:04:12.032 --> 00:04:14.778
and the biggest name in conspiracy theory exploration today.

00:04:14.864 --> 00:04:17.421
Kind of funny to think that this movie was maybe like his first

00:04:17.421 --> 00:04:18.798
conspiracy theory thing that he did.

00:04:18.884 --> 00:04:22.126
By the way, these shirts are available in my merch store link below.

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:25.086
Anyway, these eight make up the Smiley group, and they're willing to do

00:04:25.108 --> 00:04:26.714
anything to spread fear about Smiley.

00:04:26.762 --> 00:04:30.551
But making a bunch of fake viral videos will only give the character so much notoriety.

00:04:30.551 --> 00:04:33.102
So they decide that they need to actually get someone killed.

00:04:33.166 --> 00:04:36.178
Roxy has a two bedroom house near the college campus, so she puts up a

00:04:36.184 --> 00:04:39.198
listing looking for a roommate and chooses a victim out of the applicants.

00:04:39.294 --> 00:04:41.910
And they end up selecting Ashley, a new incoming student.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:45.743
Ashley is book smart, but she has no common sense, so she's easy to manipulate.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:48.215
She also has a history of mental illness in her family,

00:04:48.215 --> 00:04:50.105
and I'm guessing that the Smiley group knew about that and

00:04:50.105 --> 00:04:51.818
it contributed to them selecting her.

00:04:51.904 --> 00:04:55.146
Their first goal was to get Ashley to believe in Smiley, but what they

00:04:55.168 --> 00:04:59.201
didn't realize was that Smiley was not as made up as they thought.

00:04:59.325 --> 00:05:01.765
(IMPACT)

00:05:04.093 --> 00:05:07.950
♪ Dreadful Music ♪

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:11.646
Ashley moves in with Roxy on September 12th, one day before we see this

00:05:11.668 --> 00:05:12.362
alarm clock.

00:05:12.426 --> 00:05:14.750
A later shot makes it look like the year is 2012.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.062
Though it's kind of blurry because I was only able to get this movie on dvd.

00:05:18.126 --> 00:05:19.998
It's not available for streaming anywhere.

00:05:20.094 --> 00:05:21.042
More on that later.

00:05:21.096 --> 00:05:24.466
Ashley's dad helps her move into the new place, but she only has one box of

00:05:24.488 --> 00:05:25.582
belongings, apparently.

00:05:25.646 --> 00:05:29.158
Also, Ashley's dad looks exactly like her dad from Insidious: The Last Key,

00:05:29.164 --> 00:05:30.546
even though it's not the same actor.

00:05:30.658 --> 00:05:32.120
-[Ashley] Text me if you need anything.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.022
-[Dad] I think I'll just call.

00:05:34.156 --> 00:05:35.766
-[Ashley] Probably a good idea.

00:05:35.948 --> 00:05:39.951
Yes, because texting is too high tech for a man in his 60s in the year 2012.

00:05:39.951 --> 00:05:43.190
Like, maybe you could make that joke in like 2003, 2004.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.302
But 2012, he's not texting yet.

00:05:45.356 --> 00:05:47.414
It kind of feels like there's an intentional theme here.

00:05:47.452 --> 00:05:49.062
Like they're trying to say technology bad.

00:05:49.116 --> 00:05:50.106
Technology, dangerous.

00:05:50.178 --> 00:05:51.982
But calling is also technology.

00:05:52.116 --> 00:05:55.486
Roxy invites her new roommate to come to a party, and we see that Ashley is

00:05:55.508 --> 00:05:56.442
not very rebellious.

00:05:56.506 --> 00:05:59.066
Initially hesitant about partying on a school night, before awkwardly

00:05:59.098 --> 00:06:01.710
accepting and shaking her roommate's hand for some reason.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.999
-[Ashley] Yes, I accept your invitation.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:05.791
-[Proxy] Lovely.

00:06:05.791 --> 00:06:08.654
An interesting detail about their living situation is that Roxy

00:06:08.702 --> 00:06:11.536
apparently found her online and was stoked to meet someone normal.

00:06:11.536 --> 00:06:15.154
Again, playing into the fears of you don't know who strangers really are online.

00:06:15.272 --> 00:06:18.546
And then this immediately comes up again when Roxy claims that she's never

00:06:18.568 --> 00:06:20.014
met the people having the party.

00:06:20.142 --> 00:06:23.462
-[Proxy] No, look, I know them, okay? I just haven't met them in person.

00:06:23.596 --> 00:06:26.066
This guy from /b/ invited LA people to his house for a meetup.

00:06:26.098 --> 00:06:27.846
I guess I should probably explain what /b/ is.

00:06:27.868 --> 00:06:28.950
In case some people don't know.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.969
/b/ is an image board on the forum 4chan, where anything goes

00:06:31.969 --> 00:06:33.062
as long as it doesn't break the law.

00:06:33.116 --> 00:06:35.456
4chan, as you may know, is completely anonymous, 

00:06:35.456 --> 00:06:37.714
which feeds into the theme of online stranger danger.

00:06:37.762 --> 00:06:40.986
/b/ is the most notorious forum on 4chan, or maybe the entire Internet.

00:06:41.018 --> 00:06:44.346
It has a reputation for grotesque material, and its users are known to band

00:06:44.378 --> 00:06:47.134
together in the name of trolling, including everything from getting

00:06:47.172 --> 00:06:50.826
4chan's founder voted in as Time's Person of the Year to convincing teenagers

00:06:50.858 --> 00:06:53.646
that there was a movement to post self harm pictures in solidarity with

00:06:53.668 --> 00:06:55.242
Justin Bieber because he had cancer.

00:06:55.306 --> 00:06:57.518
Spoiler alert, he didn't have cancer.

00:06:57.614 --> 00:07:00.542
So they go into the party, and this is the party.

00:07:00.542 --> 00:07:01.951
(Crickets)

00:07:01.951 --> 00:07:04.846
I guess I shouldn't have expected much more out of a room of 4channers,

00:07:04.878 --> 00:07:06.142
but this looks very awkward.

00:07:06.206 --> 00:07:09.634
It's hosted by Zane, who meets them and immediately suggests a threesome

00:07:09.682 --> 00:07:11.462
within the first 20 seconds of knowing them.

00:07:11.516 --> 00:07:17.202
I mean, I guess he secretly knows Roxy, but, yeah, not socially adjusted.

00:07:17.266 --> 00:07:20.262
He also tells them about his area of research at the university.

00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:24.525
-[Zane] No, but really what I'm interested in is how people start believing in things

00:07:24.525 --> 00:07:27.500
you know, like Bigfoot, for example.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:33.151
There's no evidence that Bigfoot exists, yet lots of people believe in Bigfoot.

00:07:33.151 --> 00:07:37.355
This is evidence that this movie is just like the 1992 classic Candyman,

00:07:37.355 --> 00:07:39.262
except made for an audience in the digital age.

00:07:39.316 --> 00:07:43.665
In Candyman, the main character, Helen Lyle, was a graduate student doing her thesis on urban legends,

00:07:43.665 --> 00:07:46.462
and she attended a class at the beginning talking about the same topic.

00:07:46.462 --> 00:07:48.834
What causes people to believe in these stories? 

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:52.716
-[Professor] Why would Danny and Diane both be suffering

00:07:52.716 --> 00:07:56.050
from the same delusion in two cities over a thousand miles apart?

00:07:56.846 --> 00:08:01.298
See, these stories are modern oral folklore.

00:08:01.394 --> 00:08:05.618
They are the unselfconscious reflection of the fears of urban society.

00:08:05.794 --> 00:08:10.351
Also, Roxy is just inexplicably crying in this one take, which is kind of concerning.

00:08:10.351 --> 00:08:12.498
Maybe this was the first scene that they filmed, and she just

00:08:12.524 --> 00:08:15.686
had this realization that she's one of the few cast members of this movie

00:08:15.718 --> 00:08:16.854
who isn't a YouTuber.

00:08:16.902 --> 00:08:20.266
And unlike them, her career is going to be completely ruined by this.

00:08:20.368 --> 00:08:23.454
Many of the YouTubers in this movie would have their careers ruined,

00:08:23.492 --> 00:08:26.750
but being in Smiley 2012 wasn't exactly the reason behind it.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:27.822
Speaking of which.

00:08:27.956 --> 00:08:30.986
-[Proxy] Oh, hey, I think I saw that guy on campus.

00:08:31.098 --> 00:08:32.942
Yo. They did it.

00:08:33.076 --> 00:08:34.826
They actually put him in a movie.

00:08:34.938 --> 00:08:38.850
So in order to, I don't know, better sell the illusion that Smiley is real,

00:08:38.920 --> 00:08:40.962
Binder and Zane act like they don't know each other.

00:08:41.016 --> 00:08:44.178
So I guess whoever Ashley sides with can be the one to manipulate her.

00:08:44.264 --> 00:08:46.430
-[Zane] Hey, what's up? I'm Zane.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.886
-[Binder] What's up? I'm Binder.

00:08:47.998 --> 00:08:50.280
-[Zane] Yeah, what are you doing here, Pedobear?

00:08:50.973 --> 00:08:51.750
-[Binder] Don't call me that.

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:55.862
Which is very interesting foreshadowing, because in 2018 some people took

00:08:55.916 --> 00:08:59.174
podcast clips where Shane was clearly just joking around and reposted small

00:08:59.212 --> 00:09:01.874
portions of them out of context to make him look like a pedo.

00:09:01.922 --> 00:09:04.678
I'm not condoning anything he said, by the way, but it is pretty clear that

00:09:04.684 --> 00:09:06.450
the clips were edited to make him look worse.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:09.426
You can even see some of that classic Shane style of humor while promoting

00:09:09.458 --> 00:09:10.074
this movie.

00:09:10.162 --> 00:09:13.486
-[Shane] Now I have something to do on Halloween, which is go see Smiley because

00:09:13.508 --> 00:09:16.718
usually I just sit at home, I pass out candy, but technically,

00:09:16.724 --> 00:09:20.922
I'm not supposed to be within 50ft of the children so I more throw out candy.

00:09:20.986 --> 00:09:23.950
As a result of the scandalous jokes, he had to make this response video.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.234
It was a pretty big deal in the world of YouTube drama.

00:09:26.282 --> 00:09:29.358
-[Shane] I'm going to start by saying I am not a pedophile.

00:09:29.454 --> 00:09:31.471
And while he's still doing very well,

00:09:31.471 --> 00:09:33.218
his views have not been the same since this went down.

00:09:33.304 --> 00:09:37.086
Anyway, after Shane, I mean, Binder is kicked out of the party, the girls

00:09:37.118 --> 00:09:40.118
notice some guys on a website called Hide and Go Chat, which is basically an

00:09:40.124 --> 00:09:43.314
omeglesque site that pairs you up with a random stranger on video chat.

00:09:43.362 --> 00:09:45.986
Only this is not a random stranger, it's Stacy.

00:09:46.098 --> 00:09:49.362
-[Stacy] Oh no, is this that site where it just chooses who you talk with?

00:09:49.362 --> 00:09:50.749
-[Mary] Yeah. Hide and Go Chat. 

00:09:51.394 --> 00:09:52.676
-[Stacy] Yeah,

00:09:52.676 --> 00:09:54.218
you really shouldn’t be on this site.

00:09:54.384 --> 00:09:56.788
It's for people who do gross things to other people.

00:09:56.788 --> 00:10:00.230
Which was very much the case on Omegle according to its reputation.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:04.106
As I was writing this video, Omegle was actually shut down after 14 years of

00:10:04.128 --> 00:10:07.098
operation as part of a lawsuit involving child exploitation.

00:10:07.194 --> 00:10:10.286
Smiley seems to be an allegory for the potential harm that can come out of a

00:10:10.308 --> 00:10:11.946
site where users are paired anonymously.

00:10:11.978 --> 00:10:14.826
And this film isn't shy about referencing this, just like they aren't shy

00:10:14.858 --> 00:10:16.602
about the Totally Sketch Easter eggs.

00:10:16.666 --> 00:10:18.090
-[Stacy] People use this to find dates.

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:19.630
-[Mary] Yeah, a lot of guys do it.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:22.126
Some of them even do it with their pants off.

00:10:22.228 --> 00:10:24.398
-[Stacy] Okay, see, that's why you shouldn't be on the site.

00:10:24.484 --> 00:10:25.682
It's way, way too sketch.

00:10:25.746 --> 00:10:26.744
-[Mary] Totally!

00:10:26.744 --> 00:10:30.338
Crash types “I did it for the lulz” into the chat three times and someone in a

00:10:30.354 --> 00:10:32.694
Smiley mask appears behind her and pretends to stab her.

00:10:32.732 --> 00:10:35.654
I'm pretty sure there's an error in the movie because at first we see her

00:10:35.692 --> 00:10:38.294
saying “I did it for the lulz”, but it's supposed to be him saying it.

00:10:38.332 --> 00:10:39.986
And then when we cut to the wide shot, it's fixed.

00:10:40.018 --> 00:10:42.662
So it's kind of confusing as to who actually said it.

00:10:42.716 --> 00:10:45.426
And this would have been the easiest pickup shot to fix because it literally

00:10:45.458 --> 00:10:48.838
doesn't require any actors or crew, literally just a camera and a computer screen.

00:10:48.838 --> 00:10:50.206
But they didn't bother fixing it.

00:10:50.228 --> 00:10:52.766
And it's really the perfect preview of what the quality of this movie is

00:10:52.788 --> 00:10:53.470
going to be like.

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:56.798
But from Ashley's point of view, someone in a cloth Smiley mask sneaks up

00:10:56.804 --> 00:10:59.802
behind the girl on the screen and stabs her at super fast forward speed.

00:10:59.866 --> 00:11:02.446
Which doesn't make sense considering that we eventually find out that this

00:11:02.468 --> 00:11:05.450
was staged by a human and Stacy is later revealed to be alive.

00:11:05.530 --> 00:11:08.094
Meaning that what we see here was definitely faked.

00:11:08.142 --> 00:11:11.671
It seems like for the most part, the Smiley group uses these masks covered in cloth,

00:11:11.671 --> 00:11:14.546
whereas the real Smiley, who we haven't met yet, just has this

00:11:14.568 --> 00:11:16.642
smooth skin face with stitches on it.

00:11:16.696 --> 00:11:19.766
Ashley, being the unfathomably gullible freshman that she is,

00:11:19.766 --> 00:11:21.670
immediately freaks out and assumes it was real.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:22.786
-[Proxy] It's like Bloody Mary.

00:11:22.818 --> 00:11:24.002
Okay, it's an urban legend.

00:11:24.066 --> 00:11:27.138
You type “I did it for the lulz”, three times across from someone you're

00:11:27.154 --> 00:11:29.990
chatting with and then Smiley creeps into frame

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:30.490
and kills them!

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:34.026
This is another parallel to Candyman, which is also partially inspired by

00:11:34.048 --> 00:11:34.646
Bloody Mary.

00:11:34.678 --> 00:11:37.786
In Candyman, you summon him by saying his name in the mirror five times.

00:11:37.888 --> 00:11:40.922
In Smiley, you type the phrase into the chat three times to summon him.

00:11:40.976 --> 00:11:44.078
The webcam even kind of serves as a mirror because you see yourself and the

00:11:44.084 --> 00:11:45.118
person you're chatting with.

00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:48.754
And like every Candyman movie the next day, things would get real

00:11:48.754 --> 00:11:51.630
as Ashley would try summoning the evil for herself.

00:11:51.917 --> 00:11:53.917
(IMPACT)

00:11:57.326 --> 00:12:00.609
♪ Suspenseful Music ♪

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:04.338
The following day, September 13th, Ashley was late to her first class.

00:12:04.424 --> 00:12:08.338
One of her classmates is Mark, played by Toby Turner, aka Tobuscus, who used

00:12:08.344 --> 00:12:12.000
to be one of the biggest channels on YouTube for his skits, gaming, and vlogs.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.951
–[Mark] You smell better than I do.

00:12:13.951 --> 00:12:18.652
The girl he’s trying to flirt with is played by Michael Gallagher’s girlfriend and later wife, Jana Winternitz.

00:12:18.652 --> 00:12:20.914
Even the teacher has sort of a YouTube connection.

00:12:20.962 --> 00:12:24.000
He's played by Roger Bart, who is dating professional tantrum-haver

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.918
Trisha Paytas, aka blndsundoll4mj. 

00:12:27.014 --> 00:12:30.551
When Ashley gets home, Roxy informs her that a new Smiley video has surfaced.

00:12:30.551 --> 00:12:33.466
The video is titled Smiley Kills Drunk Dude, and it fills me with

00:12:33.488 --> 00:12:36.330
nostalgia to see the good old yellow subscribe button on YouTube.

00:12:36.410 --> 00:12:39.918
Anyway, we see a cloth mask version of Smiley kill off Kels, who they had

00:12:39.924 --> 00:12:41.150
met at the party last night.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.746
Ashley is scared, but Roxy suggests that they try summoning Smiley in order

00:12:44.788 --> 00:12:46.178
to find out if he's fake or not.

00:12:46.264 --> 00:12:49.990
-[Proxy] We just do it, nothing happens and then we know it's fake.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:53.816
-[Ashley[ Because that way we'll know it's fake for sure.

00:12:54.795 --> 00:12:55.295
Ok!

00:12:55.766 --> 00:12:58.546
For someone who apparently does all her homework assignments from the

00:12:58.568 --> 00:13:01.410
beginning of the semester, Ashley is really not smart.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:04.114
They scroll through hide and go chat until they get to a flasher.

00:13:04.162 --> 00:13:07.154
This is Flasher, who I mentioned earlier, and they decide to test the ritual

00:13:07.202 --> 00:13:07.782
out on him.

00:13:07.836 --> 00:13:10.678
Apparently, every character in the Smiley universe is just so horny that

00:13:10.684 --> 00:13:12.490
they literally can't think about anything else.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:14.829
Like, even after she threatens to kill him, he's just like,

00:13:14.829 --> 00:13:15.828
make out with each other.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:19.660
So Ashley types the cursed phrase three times, and then this happens.

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.703
(screams)

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:25.638
This is the most elaborate or lucky part of the plan because it had to work

00:13:25.664 --> 00:13:29.467
out to where Ashley decided to kill this particular guy, the guy who was a plant.

00:13:29.467 --> 00:13:32.126
It's possible that since the Smiley group are hackers, they were able

00:13:32.148 --> 00:13:34.846
to manipulate things on the website so that Ashley and Flasher would

00:13:34.868 --> 00:13:35.550
definitely meet.

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:39.067
Or maybe it's just their website, but that opens a whole other set of issues.

00:13:39.067 --> 00:13:40.751
How did they know that she was going to kill him? 

00:13:40.751 --> 00:13:43.551
And how did they know she wouldn't test it on her own during the day?

00:13:43.551 --> 00:13:45.478
There are no answers to these questions.

00:13:45.564 --> 00:13:47.554
It's just not a very well thought out movie.

00:13:47.602 --> 00:13:51.286
Roxy convinces Ashley not to go to the police because she claims Smiley has

00:13:51.308 --> 00:13:54.006
seen their faces, and calling the police would generate press and make it

00:13:54.028 --> 00:13:55.398
easier for the killer to find them.

00:13:55.484 --> 00:13:58.246
Basically, it seems like Roxy's making it seem as if she believes that

00:13:58.268 --> 00:13:59.894
there's nothing supernatural going on here.

00:13:59.932 --> 00:14:03.046
But if Smiley has the ability to show up behind anyone in the world who

00:14:03.068 --> 00:14:05.654
receives that message, why would they be treating him as a regular person

00:14:05.692 --> 00:14:08.067
who has to use logic and reasoning to find his victims?

00:14:08.067 --> 00:14:11.834
It's pretty clear that the only two options here are that it's staged or that it's supernatural.

00:14:11.834 --> 00:14:13.594
The next day would be September 14th.

00:14:13.642 --> 00:14:16.846
In class, Ashley draws Smiley in her notebook and writes, I did it for the lulz,

00:14:16.868 --> 00:14:20.030
three times at the top of her page, which seems like a bad idea,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:21.902
because, again, she believes this is real.

00:14:21.956 --> 00:14:24.538
And it's not a huge stretch to assume that if you can summon him by typing the phrase,

00:14:24.554 --> 00:14:26.722
you could also summon him by writing it.

00:14:26.776 --> 00:14:29.185
Later in the library, she runs into Binder again.

00:14:29.185 --> 00:14:30.951
(screams)

00:14:30.951 --> 00:14:33.278
and he, too, has more of a realistic approach.

00:14:33.374 --> 00:14:36.386
-[Binder] I think Smiley's only real when people make him real.

00:14:36.488 --> 00:14:37.538
-[Ashley] Do you think he’s real?

00:14:37.624 --> 00:14:39.362
-[Binder] I don't know if Smiley is real.

00:14:39.496 --> 00:14:41.560
I mean, I think the videos are real.

00:14:42.010 --> 00:14:43.862
That probably doesn't make any sense.

00:14:43.996 --> 00:14:45.042
No, it really doesn't.

00:14:45.106 --> 00:14:47.606
Also, they must not have had many extras for this scene, because the same

00:14:47.628 --> 00:14:49.402
people keep walking by over and over.

00:14:49.456 --> 00:14:52.118
But Ashley gets his number, and since he's playing this whole nerd character,

00:14:52.134 --> 00:14:55.020
he acts like he's literally never been asked out before.

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:55.991
-[Ashley] What’s your number?

00:14:57.909 --> 00:14:59.601
-[Binder]  Oh… ok! Oh

00:14:59.601 --> 00:15:00.990
-[Shane] It's awkward!

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:04.742
When Ashley gets home, Roxy tells her that Zane is having yet another party.

00:15:04.806 --> 00:15:07.327
This one at least looks somewhat more like what you would expect a party to

00:15:07.327 --> 00:15:07.972
look like.

00:15:07.972 --> 00:15:11.338
But don't worry, because the cinematographer makes up for it by not framing

00:15:11.354 --> 00:15:12.621
it like an actual movie.

00:15:12.621 --> 00:15:13.541
-[Proxy] Umm… here.

00:15:13.541 --> 00:15:14.393
-[Ashley] Oh I probably shouldn't. 

00:15:14.393 --> 00:15:15.951
-[Proxy] Come on, drink it! It's a free drink!

00:15:15.951 --> 00:15:18.754
I don't know what's worse, the fact that Roxy isn't facing the same

00:15:18.792 --> 00:15:22.066
direction between these two shots, or the fact that she's halfway out of the

00:15:22.168 --> 00:15:23.390
frame in her coverage.

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:26.098
Or maybe it's this guy wearing his wardrobe inside out.

00:15:26.184 --> 00:15:29.842
The scene is filled with absurd details that are completely inconsequential.

00:15:29.986 --> 00:15:31.286
-[Ashley] I think there was a bug in it.

00:15:31.308 --> 00:15:32.790
-[Proxy] I'm going to go pee in a closet.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:37.094
Okay, so later on, there is this sign on the bathroom door that says,

00:15:37.132 --> 00:15:38.802
bathroom, no peeing in closet.

00:15:38.866 --> 00:15:41.926
If we had seen this first, it might have made Roxy's joke land a little bit better,

00:15:41.948 --> 00:15:42.802
but we don't.

00:15:42.866 --> 00:15:45.606
In fact, you're unlikely to see it at all unless you're looking for it.

00:15:45.628 --> 00:15:47.554
So the joke comes off as completely random.

00:15:47.602 --> 00:15:50.874
If you weren't there in 2012, and by there, I mean on the Internet.

00:15:50.922 --> 00:15:54.317
There was very much this meme movement at the time where random equals funny.

00:15:54.317 --> 00:15:55.390
-[Kevin] I like waffles. Pff!

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:59.280
Derp. I'm like the most random person ever.

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.016
I'm like the king of randomness.

00:16:01.016 --> 00:16:02.188
Like and it's epic.

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:05.209
Potato.

00:16:05.209 --> 00:16:10.776
True! True. I think the perfect encapsulation of this era on the Internet is Nyan Cat.

00:16:10.776 --> 00:16:13.650
It came out in 2011, and there's no story behind why.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.466
It's just a cat whose body is a pop tart running through space while

00:16:16.488 --> 00:16:17.853
emitting a rainbow trail.

00:16:17.853 --> 00:16:20.833
While this absolute bop of an 8-bit vocaloid song plays.

00:16:20.833 --> 00:16:22.878
♪ Nyan Cat Music ♪

00:16:22.878 --> 00:16:26.118
But where Nyan Cat was loved then and remembered fondly today,

00:16:26.124 --> 00:16:28.742
I have a feeling Smiley's humor was cringe even when it came out.

00:16:28.796 --> 00:16:31.117
And seriously, how do you miss the framing this badly?

00:16:31.117 --> 00:16:32.530
Half her head is out of the frame.

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:35.674
Was nobody looking at the monitor? Did the camera operator stitch his eyes shut

00:16:35.712 --> 00:16:37.717
after seeing the quality of the acting?

00:16:37.717 --> 00:16:40.554
Ashley had come to the party hoping to get her mind off of Smiley.

00:16:40.602 --> 00:16:43.303
But that doesn't last long as she overhears a conversation

00:16:43.303 --> 00:16:44.867
about yet another victim.

00:16:44.867 --> 00:16:48.252
(IMPACT)

00:16:49.718 --> 00:16:53.650
♪ Dreadful Music ♪

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.978
While Roxy is in the bathroom, Ashley goes over to say hi to Zane,

00:16:56.984 --> 00:17:00.542
who is hanging out with Crash and Decepticon, hoping to maybe find some answers.

00:17:00.686 --> 00:17:02.370
-[Zane] Hey what’s up?

00:17:02.623 --> 00:17:04.294
-[Ashley] Hey, I’m Ashley. 

00:17:04.294 --> 00:17:07.644
-[Zane] Yeah, yeah, I know. We met the other night.

00:17:07.644 --> 00:17:11.094
You wanted to have sex with me, but I wasn't into it.

00:17:11.212 --> 00:17:11.751
-[Ashley] What?

00:17:11.751 --> 00:17:13.099
-[Zane] Hmmm...

00:17:14.518 --> 00:17:18.598
Yo! Oh my God! Bro just dropped the funniest joke of all time.

00:17:18.684 --> 00:17:20.022
Oh, you got her, man.

00:17:20.076 --> 00:17:20.742
You got her.

00:17:20.796 --> 00:17:22.214
That one was so funny.

00:17:22.342 --> 00:17:25.306
This was my least favorite era of pop culture, by the way.

00:17:25.408 --> 00:17:28.310
Basically, they laugh off her concerns about their missing cohort.

00:17:28.390 --> 00:17:30.314
-[Crash] He's probably just pulling a freaking prank on us.

00:17:30.352 --> 00:17:32.250
Hello? They haven't even found the body.

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:33.717
-[Ashley] Well, do you know where he lives? 

00:17:33.717 --> 00:17:34.771
-[Crash] I don't know.

00:17:35.076 --> 00:17:36.919
-[Decepticon] Probably with his mom.

00:17:36.919 --> 00:17:39.543
-[Crash] Dude, did you just come up with that?

00:17:39.543 --> 00:17:40.874
Wow. What an epic joke. Dude.

00:17:40.922 --> 00:17:43.578
There's no way you came up with that on the spot.

00:17:43.674 --> 00:17:46.862
It seems kind of counterintuitive for them to mock her, showing actual

00:17:46.916 --> 00:17:50.274
concern about the video being real, considering that they are trying to get

00:17:50.312 --> 00:17:51.018
her to believe that it's all real.

00:17:51.018 --> 00:17:53.751
Like, that's their main objective here, right?

00:17:53.751 --> 00:17:55.650
Maybe it's like a reverse psychology thing.

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:58.782
Like she'll think it's more real if she's the only one taking it seriously.

00:17:58.846 --> 00:18:02.438
I feel like that would only work on an idiot, which she is, but still.

00:18:02.524 --> 00:18:06.214
Crash even flaunts the truth before her, but she's unable to pick up on it.

00:18:06.332 --> 00:18:10.230
-[Crash] Maybe it’s the end of the Truman Show and your boat just hit the sky mural.

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:15.306
Zane pulls her aside, and they cut to a shot that's out of focus as he

00:18:15.328 --> 00:18:18.842
interrogates her on what she saw, where he's again out of focus,

00:18:18.896 --> 00:18:20.576
then accuses her of summoning Smiley,

00:18:20.576 --> 00:18:22.628
which he only knows about because he was in on the plan.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:25.898
He tells her that he has summoned the grinning antagonist as well,

00:18:25.914 --> 00:18:28.190
claiming that Smiley appeared and sliced up a random kid.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:31.486
This makes Ashley sick to her stomach, and she decides to go home.

00:18:31.588 --> 00:18:35.751
But before she leaves, Zane continues to demonstrate the worst timing known to man.

00:18:35.751 --> 00:18:37.717
-[Zane] You can sleepover.

00:18:37.717 --> 00:18:40.482
Which is a weird play to make on someone who's throwing up to begin with,

00:18:40.536 --> 00:18:43.538
especially when he just slammed her with that joke when he said he

00:18:43.544 --> 00:18:44.962
wasn't interested in sleeping with her.

00:18:45.016 --> 00:18:47.422
There was just so much weird going on in this movie.

00:18:47.486 --> 00:18:50.434
On my Letterboxd, I wrote that this movie was a modern day Candyman.

00:18:50.482 --> 00:18:54.166
If Candyman embarrassed itself and everyone involved every five minutes,

00:18:54.188 --> 00:18:56.886
and that really is true, I'm struggling with what I should include in this video,

00:18:56.908 --> 00:18:59.010
but that's too weird not to include.

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:00.774
Like, for example, she's going home.

00:19:00.812 --> 00:19:03.686
And they use this editing technique to make it look like she's drunk and she

00:19:03.708 --> 00:19:05.830
confuses some other person for Roxy.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:06.967
-[Ashley] Roxy...

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:10.010
But that literally looks nothing like Roxy.

00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:13.402
Be Ashley was only at that party for three minutes and 58 seconds.

00:19:13.466 --> 00:19:14.446
Yes. I timed it.

00:19:14.468 --> 00:19:17.294
If we look back, her roommate spilled her drink immediately and then gave

00:19:17.332 --> 00:19:19.886
her this other drink, which she took one sip of, then claims that there was

00:19:19.908 --> 00:19:20.494
a bug in it.

00:19:20.532 --> 00:19:24.771
So I guess the implication is that she was roofied and it was Roxy and Zane's plan all along

00:19:24.771 --> 00:19:28.826
to roofy her and have her encounter Smiley on the way home. especially considering this line...

00:19:29.067 --> 00:19:32.433
-[Zane] What if he comes for the people who call him? Have you thought about that?

00:19:32.433 --> 00:19:34.366
So, of course, that does happen,

00:19:34.388 --> 00:19:36.694
even though that wasn't heard of the lore at any point before this.

00:19:36.732 --> 00:19:39.906
But at first, this seems to be the real Smiley, based on the fact that she's

00:19:39.938 --> 00:19:43.834
approached by this scary figure with the smooth skinned face and stitched on features.

00:19:43.834 --> 00:19:46.598
As far as we know, this is the first time that the legend that the

00:19:46.604 --> 00:19:48.198
group has created has become real.

00:19:48.284 --> 00:19:51.654
So she runs home, and inside she's attacked by a fake Smiley,

00:19:51.692 --> 00:19:53.222
someone wearing the cloth Smiley mask.

00:19:53.286 --> 00:19:54.806
This is all needlessly confusing.

00:19:54.838 --> 00:19:57.754
And on top of that, he grabs her, and she wakes up in the bed the next morning,

00:19:57.792 --> 00:19:59.818
still screaming for some reason.

00:19:59.904 --> 00:20:02.714
I'm going to try to look past the insanity that has been the last

00:20:02.752 --> 00:20:04.522
five minutes of this movie and move on.

00:20:04.576 --> 00:20:08.346
September 15th, Roxy comes in and tries to calm her down again, but Ashley

00:20:08.378 --> 00:20:11.054
ends up seeing the school psychiatrist, where we learn that she used to take

00:20:11.092 --> 00:20:12.842
medication for bipolar disorder.

00:20:12.906 --> 00:20:15.886
She now seems convinced that her encounter with Smiley was a dream.

00:20:15.918 --> 00:20:18.498
So I guess Roxy's dismissive attitude actually worked.

00:20:18.584 --> 00:20:21.806
So the doctor writes her a prescription for Ativan to relieve anxiety.

00:20:21.918 --> 00:20:23.380
-[Ashley] I just remembered something.

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:25.554
I'm not crazy.

00:20:25.672 --> 00:20:26.740
-[Doctor] Okay, good.

00:20:27.430 --> 00:20:31.654
Yeah. Immediately after leaving the psychiatrist's office, she's greeted by Roxy,

00:20:31.692 --> 00:20:33.666
who delivers news about a new Smiley victim.

00:20:33.698 --> 00:20:35.622
Number four, if you're keeping count.

00:20:35.676 --> 00:20:39.314
So they go back to Zane's, again, and he's waving around a gun and

00:20:39.362 --> 00:20:41.122
reveals he's not a college student, he's a hacker.

00:20:41.186 --> 00:20:43.722
Which really doesn't explain why he's waving around a gun,

00:20:43.722 --> 00:20:45.258
but he seems to believe that it does.

00:20:45.424 --> 00:20:46.232
-[Zane] Who's doing this?

00:20:46.232 --> 00:20:47.082
-[Proxy] We don't know.

00:20:47.634 --> 00:20:52.151
-[Zane] Okay, well, why don't you riddle me this? Who is she? 

00:20:52.324 --> 00:20:54.976
-[Ashley] Me? Smiley's trying to kill me.

00:20:54.976 --> 00:20:56.286
-[Zane] Smiley's trying to kill me.

00:20:56.388 --> 00:20:58.830
Dude, that shot was not even close to being in focus.

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:00.650
You cannot just use that in a movie.

00:21:00.730 --> 00:21:03.786
Zane is worried, or at least he's acting like he's worried that whoever's

00:21:03.818 --> 00:21:06.046
behind this is one of the enemies of his hacking group.

00:21:06.148 --> 00:21:11.401
-[Zane] You don't get to be king of the Internet a**holes without making a few enemies.

00:21:11.401 --> 00:21:14.313
Which seems like a reference to the tagline of the movie The Social Network,

00:21:14.313 --> 00:21:16.786
which is embarrassing because The Social Network is probably the

00:21:16.808 --> 00:21:19.858
best movie ever made about the Internet, and this may be the worst.

00:21:19.954 --> 00:21:22.978
He lists off some potential enemies, one of which is the feds.

00:21:23.074 --> 00:21:25.058
-[Zane] The feds could be the FBI.

00:21:25.154 --> 00:21:26.230
That makes no sense.

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:28.971
Why would the FBI go around killing college age kids?

00:21:28.971 --> 00:21:30.612
If they did something to with the FBI,

00:21:30.612 --> 00:21:32.918
I'm pretty sure the FBI would just arrest them.

00:21:32.924 --> 00:21:35.766
In the night, she has a dream about a woman sitting by her window, which I think is

00:21:35.788 --> 00:21:38.486
supposed to be her dead mom, but when she approaches, it turns out to be Smiley.

00:21:38.508 --> 00:21:41.914
But this one seems to actually be a dream, which really just muddies

00:21:41.962 --> 00:21:42.766
what happened before.

00:21:42.868 --> 00:21:45.610
I guess maybe that's the intention to just throw off the audience.

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:48.254
However, I feel like a good red herring will make sense at the end.

00:21:48.292 --> 00:21:50.014
Like, you can look back at it and say, oh, yeah.

00:21:50.052 --> 00:21:51.982
Now I understand why we were led to believe this.

00:21:52.036 --> 00:21:53.310
Not the case with Smiley.

00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:56.994
What happened next, however, seems to offer a possible explanation for how

00:21:57.032 --> 00:21:57.826
Smiley came to be.

00:21:57.848 --> 00:22:00.690
And I think there's something that everyone overlooked.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:03.629
(IMPACT)

00:22:05.126 --> 00:22:09.350
♪ Suspenseful Music ♪

00:22:09.350 --> 00:22:12.582
We pick back up in another class, and it seems to be the following week.

00:22:12.636 --> 00:22:13.942
It says Thursday on the board.

00:22:13.996 --> 00:22:17.126
So I guess we're picking back up on September 20th, 2012.

00:22:17.228 --> 00:22:18.118
Let's go with that.

00:22:18.204 --> 00:22:21.546
They're now talking about philosophy, which brings up another good question.

00:22:21.648 --> 00:22:25.951
What is this class? It's called intro to reason and ethics, and it's required.

00:22:25.951 --> 00:22:29.350
Last week, it was about the scientific method and now philosophy.

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:32.214
They're talking about Occam's Razor, or the principle that the simplest explanation

00:22:32.262 --> 00:22:35.822
is usually correct and Tobuscus gets something right for once.

00:22:35.956 --> 00:22:39.151
-[Professor] Now, what is the implication of what I just said?

00:22:39.151 --> 00:22:43.838
-[Mark] It's almost like everything that happened, happened so we could know it happened.

00:22:43.924 --> 00:22:47.226
What he's describing is the anthropic principle, the idea that the universe

00:22:47.258 --> 00:22:50.346
seems to be fine tuned to support the existence of life, even though it's

00:22:50.378 --> 00:22:51.194
very unlikely.

00:22:51.242 --> 00:22:54.462
If the laws of the universe were slightly different, there would be no life.

00:22:54.516 --> 00:22:57.270
And if there is no life, there would be nobody to observe this fact.

00:22:57.340 --> 00:23:00.566
It's almost as if we, as intelligent life, are supposed to notice that the

00:23:00.588 --> 00:23:02.598
universe operates in a way that supports life.

00:23:02.684 --> 00:23:04.662
I will give the movie a little bit of credit here.

00:23:04.716 --> 00:23:07.686
This is a clever lesson to have Ashley sitting in on, because all of the

00:23:07.708 --> 00:23:10.854
scenarios that the Smiley group have set up were set up specifically to make

00:23:10.892 --> 00:23:11.906
her believe in Smiley.

00:23:11.938 --> 00:23:14.602
It's all a huge prank on Ashley, but she doesn't notice it.

00:23:14.656 --> 00:23:17.606
She does, however, have an interesting conclusion about the anthropic principle

00:23:17.638 --> 00:23:19.014
during this lesson.

00:23:19.062 --> 00:23:23.246
-[Ashley] It assumes we're the reason, when really we could just be amoebas or

00:23:23.268 --> 00:23:26.560
whatever simple forms of life were necessary for us to develop.

00:23:28.690 --> 00:23:32.334
We might be what's necessary for something else to develop.

00:23:32.452 --> 00:23:35.682
And her teacher points out that this may have already started to happen.

00:23:35.816 --> 00:23:39.986
-[Professor] Humanity may be nothing more than an intermediate step in the development of

00:23:40.008 --> 00:23:42.530
a consciousness a trillion times greater.

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:45.421
We already built it a network.

00:23:45.421 --> 00:23:47.622
Millions of nodes communicating with each other.

00:23:47.676 --> 00:23:49.270
It is called the Internet.

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:55.558
Let's just hope that when it becomes conscious, it's nice to us.

00:23:55.644 --> 00:23:57.794
Which I think is actually an interesting concept.

00:23:57.842 --> 00:24:00.790
It's probably not for this movie, but maybe something like M3GAN.

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:03.766
Tobuscus and his girlfriend even bring up a couple of fictional examples

00:24:03.798 --> 00:24:05.270
like Terminator and The Matrix.

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:08.266
It seems as if this scene is almost here to suggest that Smiley is the

00:24:08.288 --> 00:24:10.022
incarnation of that higher consciousness.

00:24:10.086 --> 00:24:13.034
The Internet is a series of interconnected users, and we've already seen

00:24:13.072 --> 00:24:14.902
several examples of how that can be harmful.

00:24:14.966 --> 00:24:17.438
Not to mention that the group of Internet trolls that come together to

00:24:17.444 --> 00:24:20.318
create the fake Smiley are all from the /b/ board on 4chan,

00:24:20.324 --> 00:24:23.114
which is infamous for harnessing the power of many to troll and do harm.

00:24:23.162 --> 00:24:26.351
Like I mentioned with the Time magazine poll and the Justin Bieber cutting thing.

00:24:26.351 --> 00:24:30.517
It could be that the real Smiley is the supernatural incarnation of that concept.

00:24:30.517 --> 00:24:33.106
The same way that Candyman was the supernatural incarnation of

00:24:33.128 --> 00:24:34.830
the hurt caused by racial segregation.

00:24:34.910 --> 00:24:37.938
But Candyman was covered in bees and had a hook for a hand because of the

00:24:37.944 --> 00:24:39.594
torture that he endured when he was killed.

00:24:39.662 --> 00:24:43.222
So why does Smiley have a smooth face and permanently stitched smile?

00:24:43.276 --> 00:24:45.654
Well, sometimes the simplest explanation is the correct one.

00:24:45.692 --> 00:24:48.397
What if it's just supposed to represent a smiley face emoji?

00:24:48.397 --> 00:24:51.601
Emoji are kind of a symbol of the Internet, and that would kind of explain

00:24:51.601 --> 00:24:52.866
why you summon him by typing.

00:24:52.898 --> 00:24:53.974
I did it for the lulz.

00:24:54.022 --> 00:24:56.394
Like, it's the stupidest thing ever, but it kinda makes sense.

00:24:56.512 --> 00:24:58.570
Binder even kind of hints at this later on.

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:01.626
-[Binder] Do you know what this means? I thought I was crazy.

00:25:01.808 --> 00:25:06.757
But if this is true, if you're not lying, then that means something's been awakened, like something's been born. 

00:25:06.757 --> 00:25:07.868
Like, Smiley could be the next generation.

00:25:07.868 --> 00:25:08.715
-[Ashley] Are you all listening to me? 

00:25:08.964 --> 00:25:10.794
I really kind of like this whole discussion,

00:25:10.842 --> 00:25:13.534
how it ties into the false narrative that the 4channers are creating for

00:25:13.572 --> 00:25:16.334
Ashley and how it represents what Smiley actually is.

00:25:16.452 --> 00:25:20.548
The problem is that it's just too smart for this movie because inside the classroom,

00:25:20.548 --> 00:25:22.562
Ashley is smart, and she's the one who put this together.

00:25:22.616 --> 00:25:25.182
But then outside the classroom, she's a complete smooth brain.

00:25:25.246 --> 00:25:28.146
So, yeah, there's an interesting concept buried in here somewhere, and

00:25:28.168 --> 00:25:30.694
nobody noticed it because most were too busy laughing at it.

00:25:30.732 --> 00:25:32.342
And at times, it's hard to blame them.

00:25:32.396 --> 00:25:36.246
Just remember that Smiley was the original Emoji Movie five years before the

00:25:36.268 --> 00:25:37.042
Emoji Movie.

00:25:37.106 --> 00:25:40.086
Also, when I googled the Emoji Movie to double check what year it came out,

00:25:40.108 --> 00:25:43.047
the first thing that caught my eye was this cast list icon

00:25:43.047 --> 00:25:44.232
Patrick Stewart... Poop.

00:25:44.232 --> 00:25:47.146
Which is pretty funny if you think immature humor is funny,

00:25:47.168 --> 00:25:47.930
which I absolutely do.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.162
Anyway, let's move on.

00:25:49.216 --> 00:25:52.998
Ashley goes to the library and finally googles Smiley urban legend and finds

00:25:53.014 --> 00:25:56.354
a video from a little girl whose babysitter was supposedly killed by Smiley.

00:25:56.422 --> 00:25:59.646
We see what appears to be Smiley come up and poke her with a knife,

00:25:59.668 --> 00:26:00.938
but it turns out to just be Binder.

00:26:01.034 --> 00:26:02.202
But he doesn't have a knife.

00:26:02.202 --> 00:26:06.226
I get that this is our foreshadowing clue that he's Smiley and that he was behind it all,

00:26:06.226 --> 00:26:08.706
but I'm not sure what I, as an audience member, am supposed

00:26:08.728 --> 00:26:10.546
to take away from that in a literal sense.

00:26:10.648 --> 00:26:14.146
When she admits that she performed the ritual and saw someone die, his

00:26:14.168 --> 00:26:18.807
reaction sounds like one of those old, edgy podcast clips that got him canceled a few years ago.

00:26:19.062 --> 00:26:20.420
-[Ashley] I killed someone? 

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:22.726
-[Binder] Are you serious?! Oh that’s amazing.

00:26:22.828 --> 00:26:25.606
I mean that’s terrible obviously, but I tried to do it

00:26:25.628 --> 00:26:26.128
and nothing happened.

00:26:26.156 --> 00:26:29.026
She goes home and clicks through a bunch of strangers on hide and go chat.

00:26:29.058 --> 00:26:32.230
For some reason, as usual, it's just filled with horny people.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:35.226
Until she comes across someone sitting in the dark who asks if he should

00:26:35.248 --> 00:26:37.770
visit her again and claims to be standing behind her.

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:41.098
When she turns around, she sees Roxy, who berates her for going back on the website.

00:26:41.104 --> 00:26:44.394
This is another kind of foreshadowing clue because Roxy is also

00:26:44.432 --> 00:26:45.870
secretly part of the Smiley group.

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:49.006
She tries calling her dad for comfort, but Smiley's voice seems to take over

00:26:49.028 --> 00:26:49.528
her phone.

00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:52.654
So she returns to the psychiatrist, admits what she really thinks she saw,

00:26:52.692 --> 00:26:55.322
and they plan to meet the following day to check her into a clinic.

00:26:55.386 --> 00:26:57.710
An office visit with her teacher isn't much help either.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:26:59.130
Turns out he's an alcoholic.

00:26:59.210 --> 00:27:01.886
I'm not really sure what that adds to the story, but they make a point to

00:27:01.908 --> 00:27:02.462
highlight it.

00:27:02.516 --> 00:27:05.586
Maybe this is too much of a nitpick, but as the teacher explains his views

00:27:05.618 --> 00:27:07.842
on evil, they're locked face to face in conversation.

00:27:07.906 --> 00:27:10.726
And we see these profile shots of each of them, but it's just super off

00:27:10.748 --> 00:27:14.034
putting to me how their heads are centered in the frame rather than their faces.

00:27:14.034 --> 00:27:16.854
Usually as a director, when you compose a frame, the negative space

00:27:16.892 --> 00:27:18.630
is in the direction that the character is looking.

00:27:18.700 --> 00:27:20.482
Like this profile shot in Candyman.

00:27:20.546 --> 00:27:23.718
By framing their heads like this, the result is this shot where the focal

00:27:23.734 --> 00:27:25.866
point is Ashley's ear instead of her face.

00:27:25.968 --> 00:27:28.330
Anyway, she gets freaked out when he says this.

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:33.310
-[Professor] What is it that the kids say? It's the perfect expression of nihilism.

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:36.000
I did it for the...

00:27:36.770 --> 00:27:38.746
-[Ashley] I did it for the lulz.

00:27:38.938 --> 00:27:41.280
-[Professor] Yeah, that's it.

00:27:42.130 --> 00:27:43.690
I did it for the lulz.

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:45.667
How are we supposed to take this seriously?

00:27:45.667 --> 00:27:47.002
Later, she's in the library

00:27:47.066 --> 00:27:49.822
working on a paper when she hears weird growling in her headphones.

00:27:49.886 --> 00:27:53.867
And Smiley, that is the fake mask version of Smiley, suddenly appears on her screen.

00:27:53.867 --> 00:27:56.786
And she responds by destroying her entire computer rather than just

00:27:56.808 --> 00:27:58.782
turning it off or doing a factory reset.

00:27:58.846 --> 00:28:02.278
Making our usual pair of extras who are in every library scene think that

00:28:02.284 --> 00:28:06.151
she's insane and some random skater boy records her breakdown and puts it online.

00:28:06.151 --> 00:28:08.690
This is actually a director's cameo from Michael Gallagher.

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:11.590
Then in what I'm assuming is another night because she's wearing completely

00:28:11.660 --> 00:28:12.418
different clothes.

00:28:12.514 --> 00:28:15.414
But I wouldn't put it past this movie to not have the wardrobe department

00:28:15.462 --> 00:28:16.090
together either.

00:28:16.160 --> 00:28:19.178
Ashley is jump scared when someone in the Smiley mask appears behind her in the mirror,

00:28:19.184 --> 00:28:21.014
but he immediately disappears.

00:28:21.142 --> 00:28:24.438
Since this person is wearing the cloth Smiley mask, but clearly demonstrates

00:28:24.454 --> 00:28:27.482
the ability to disappear, I'm guessing this was just her imagination.

00:28:27.546 --> 00:28:30.474
But again, with this movie, there's always the option that it was a mistake

00:28:30.522 --> 00:28:32.142
and they just used the wrong mask or something.

00:28:32.196 --> 00:28:35.394
I'm gonna assume it was Ashley starting to lose her mind because after this,

00:28:35.432 --> 00:28:38.020
she would be driven to a mental breaking point.

00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:41.052
(IMPACT)

00:28:43.491 --> 00:28:47.259
♪ Dreadful Music ♪

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:51.602
That night, dark dreams cloud Ashley's head once again, this time involving

00:28:51.666 --> 00:28:55.106
multiple Smiley masked killers in her room and imagery of her mother turning

00:28:55.138 --> 00:28:56.822
into Smiley to drool blood on her.

00:28:56.876 --> 00:28:59.878
Upon awakening, she poses the question that we've all been wondering for the

00:28:59.884 --> 00:29:00.882
last while now.

00:29:00.956 --> 00:29:05.062
-[Ashley] Is this real or am I just going crazy?
-[Proxy] I think both dude.

00:29:05.126 --> 00:29:08.906
So she finally decides to call the police and

00:29:08.928 --> 00:29:10.010
they predictably don't take her seriously at all.

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:15.802
-[Diamond] I'm gonna tell you the part I'm having a hard time with... all of it!

00:29:15.936 --> 00:29:19.006
Based! He does mention that they've received a few calls about Smiley and asked her

00:29:19.028 --> 00:29:21.726
to consider if she's on the wrong end of some kind of prank,

00:29:21.748 --> 00:29:23.210
which she angrily refuses to entertain.

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:26.158
They have also seen the video of her smashing her laptop, which has been

00:29:26.164 --> 00:29:28.650
uploaded as hot girl goes crazy in library.

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:31.754
Now remember, the one who recorded this video was the Michael Gallagher insert.

00:29:31.802 --> 00:29:34.966
So is he making fun of his own channel, where all of his most

00:29:35.028 --> 00:29:37.734
popular videos have one thing in common? And if you're listening to this audio only,

00:29:37.772 --> 00:29:40.454
you can look at the screen right now to see what that one thing is.

00:29:40.492 --> 00:29:44.118
Or does Michael actually see this as unironically how to do YouTube?

00:29:44.124 --> 00:29:47.686
The timing of this is also super interesting because Smiley came out in 2012.

00:29:47.788 --> 00:29:50.806
That was the year YouTube changed its algorithm to focus on watch time in

00:29:50.828 --> 00:29:53.846
order to help combat clickbait and promote good content that keeps viewers engaged.

00:29:53.878 --> 00:29:56.250
There's also another mistake right here.

00:29:56.320 --> 00:29:58.182
These are the old YouTube editing features.

00:29:58.246 --> 00:30:00.938
They should have been logged out to record this because we're seeing this on

00:30:00.944 --> 00:30:02.138
the police detective's computer.

00:30:02.224 --> 00:30:04.314
They would not be logged into Michael's YouTube account.

00:30:04.432 --> 00:30:07.838
This is another error that would be easy to fix in editing... like this.

00:30:07.924 --> 00:30:08.970
But they didn't bother.

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:12.446
As a last resort, Ashley meets with Binder, who gives her an old computer of

00:30:12.468 --> 00:30:14.474
his that he set up with all of the data encrypted.

00:30:14.522 --> 00:30:17.326
And even though he's still playing the part of the self pitying loser,

00:30:17.348 --> 00:30:18.730
she makes a move on him and they kiss.

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:21.594
Roxy also claims that she's going away to her parents' house for the weekend

00:30:21.642 --> 00:30:22.334
to clear her head.

00:30:22.372 --> 00:30:24.670
So the stage is set for the final step of their plan.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:25.922
Onec night has fallen,

00:30:25.986 --> 00:30:28.854
Roxy video chats her in a panic, thinking that something may have happened

00:30:28.892 --> 00:30:30.674
to Zane, who isn't answering her calls.

00:30:30.722 --> 00:30:33.142
Ashley volunteers to go over to his place to check on him.

00:30:33.196 --> 00:30:36.118
How convenient that there was just a flashlight laying on the floor right in

00:30:36.124 --> 00:30:37.202
front of the unlocked door.

00:30:37.266 --> 00:30:39.266
Not that you actually tried turning on the lights.

00:30:39.298 --> 00:30:42.326
I mean, the patio lights seem to work just fine, so there's no reason to

00:30:42.348 --> 00:30:43.574
believe that the power is out.

00:30:43.612 --> 00:30:45.650
Not to mention that there's music playing in the distance.

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:48.742
Seriously, why don't you just try the light switch once?

00:30:48.796 --> 00:30:50.814
She finds the words I did it for the lulz graffitied across the wall.

00:30:50.852 --> 00:30:53.722
And she discovers Zane apparently covered in blood on the floor.

00:30:53.786 --> 00:30:56.202
Don't worry, he's not dead and it's not real blood.

00:30:56.266 --> 00:30:57.390
So I can show it.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:31:00.446
Also, why would you scream when you see the body? The killer could easily be

00:31:00.468 --> 00:31:02.814
standing nearby, and you'd just be calling attention to yourself.

00:31:02.932 --> 00:31:06.286
She finds all of the monitors in his living room kind of area glitching out

00:31:06.308 --> 00:31:08.226
with a video of the Smiley mask behind it all.

00:31:08.248 --> 00:31:11.474
So she starts destroying them. Because that worked so well last time right?

00:31:11.512 --> 00:31:14.914
She also grabs Zane's gun, which has seemingly been left out for her.

00:31:14.952 --> 00:31:15.950
Much like the flashlight.

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:19.166
It seems a little dangerous to give the person with a history of psychiatric issues a gun,

00:31:19.198 --> 00:31:22.502
especially when you're actively trying to make her go crazy

00:31:22.556 --> 00:31:24.162
through an elaborate prank that you're pulling.

00:31:24.226 --> 00:31:26.886
I'm gonna give Zane the benefit of the doubt and assume that the gun is

00:31:26.908 --> 00:31:28.562
filled with blanks and not real bullets.

00:31:28.626 --> 00:31:30.694
But it still seems like more trouble than it's worth.

00:31:30.812 --> 00:31:34.358
She returns home to the video chat with Roxy and asks her to type,

00:31:34.364 --> 00:31:34.994
I did it for the lulz.

00:31:35.042 --> 00:31:38.134
The idea is that they'll summon Smiley behind her, and then Ashley can turn

00:31:38.172 --> 00:31:39.362
around and shoot him with the gun.

00:31:39.426 --> 00:31:42.974
If Smiley is real, this is a terrible plan because we literally just

00:31:43.012 --> 00:31:46.202
established that Zane had the gun and he was still unable to overcome Smiley.

00:31:46.266 --> 00:31:49.646
So they go through with it, and Ashley sees someone coming in

00:31:49.668 --> 00:31:52.062
through the front door and fires as soon as he opens it.

00:31:52.116 --> 00:31:54.266
Only she finds out that this wasn't Smiley.

00:31:54.298 --> 00:31:56.170
It was her knight in shining armor Binder.

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:58.974
Never mind the idea that he immediately just tries to enter the house

00:31:59.012 --> 00:32:01.886
without knocking or calling, even though they've only known each other for

00:32:01.908 --> 00:32:04.190
at this point, like, three weeks and aren't that close yet.

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:07.154
How they accomplish just their plan is never explicitly detailed.

00:32:07.202 --> 00:32:10.118
But like I said, I'm guessing the gun was filled with blanks and Binder was

00:32:10.124 --> 00:32:12.342
wearing a squib so he could make it look like she had shot him.

00:32:12.396 --> 00:32:15.334
Honestly, I'm going to get into how this plan is terrible and would only

00:32:15.372 --> 00:32:17.254
work if absolutely everything goes right.

00:32:17.292 --> 00:32:18.774
But let's see the plot through to the end first.

00:32:18.812 --> 00:32:22.038
Ashley tries to tend to Binder, but that's when someone in a Smiley mask

00:32:22.054 --> 00:32:24.582
appears from behind him and slits his throat.

00:32:24.646 --> 00:32:25.882
Or so it seems.

00:32:26.016 --> 00:32:28.474
She probably could have seen him approaching, but whatever.

00:32:28.592 --> 00:32:31.466
She's chased through the house up to her room, where she locks herself in

00:32:31.488 --> 00:32:34.518
and waits with the gun drawn, only to realize that someone in a Smiley mask

00:32:34.534 --> 00:32:37.130
is in the room with her already and she's all out of bullets.

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:40.126
She's cornered by several more Smiley mask killers, and in an act of desperation,

00:32:40.148 --> 00:32:43.854
she jumps through an absolutely terrible looking CGI window and falls to her death,

00:32:43.892 --> 00:32:46.602
or at least what appears to be her death.

00:32:46.666 --> 00:32:48.922
It's not that high, but she hit her head on the ground.

00:32:49.066 --> 00:32:52.142
Boys, we !@#$% did it.

00:32:52.196 --> 00:32:54.686
This is kind of what establishes that they wanted to kill her the whole time.

00:32:54.708 --> 00:32:57.846
I don't know why they wanted to kill her and I don't know why they

00:32:57.868 --> 00:33:00.310
didn't just throw her out the window in order to accomplish that.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:03.734
I get that they are 4chan  posters and they like chaos or whatever,

00:33:03.772 --> 00:33:06.758
but it seems like if they wanted to spread the legend of Smiley,

00:33:06.764 --> 00:33:08.338
Ashley could do that for them if she had survived.

00:33:08.434 --> 00:33:11.202
Also, it's not clear what this has to do with Anonymous.

00:33:11.266 --> 00:33:13.686
They're actually worried that the main faction of the hacking group won't

00:33:13.718 --> 00:33:14.602
approve of their message.

00:33:14.656 --> 00:33:17.818
But Binder argues that because Anonymous is anonymous that they should get

00:33:17.824 --> 00:33:19.738
as much say in the message as anyone else.

00:33:19.824 --> 00:33:24.094
